{"blocks":[["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-241","Summary",["Music as a Gradual Process"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-255","A short set of observations and aphoristic statements by Person/Steve Reich, written in New Mexico in the summer of 1968 and first published in the catalogue to the exhibition Anti-Illusion: Procedures/Materials at the Whitney Museum of American Art in 1969.",["Music as a Gradual Process","Summary"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-524","Reich writes about pieces of music that are themselves processes, which should be audible and should happen extremely gradually. He contrasts them with Person/John Cage's chance procedures and with serial music, where the process can't be heard in the sounding music.",["Music as a Gradual Process","Summary"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-800","Reprinted in Reich's Writings on Music, 1965–2000 (Oxford University Press, 2002), pp. 34–36. The whole book is also in Reader: Writings on Music, 1965–2000.",["Music as a Gradual Process","Summary"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-1030","Notes",["Music as a Gradual Process"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-1042","What \"process\" means in the essay",["Music as a Gradual Process","Notes"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-1347","Control and acceptance",["Music as a Gradual Process","Notes"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-1572","Improvisation",["Music as a Gradual Process","Notes"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-1982","The phrase \"no room for improvisation\" is Christensen's paraphrase of Reich (2003), quoted in Erik Schoster's paper Where do you place agency in a process-based music system. Reich's own sentence is the one above.",["Music as a Gradual Process","Notes","Improvisation"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-2261","What remains outside the composer's intentions",["Music as a Gradual Process","Notes"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-2616","Highlights",["Music as a Gradual Process"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-2801","Readwise",["Music as a Gradual Process","Highlights"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Essay/68/Music%20as%20a%20Gradual%20Process/","source-block-3128","Readwise",["Music as a Gradual Process","Highlights"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Music/Work/Variations%20for%20Winds%2C%20Strings%2C%20and%20Keyboards/","source-block-139","Variations for Winds, Strings, and Keyboards",[]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Music/Work/Variations%20for%20Winds%2C%20Strings%2C%20and%20Keyboards/","source-block-188","Steve Reich's orchestral work is scored for winds, brass, strings, and keyboards. Reich's catalog also lists an ensemble version under the same work title.",["Variations for Winds, Strings, and Keyboards"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Music/Work/Variations%20for%20Winds%2C%20Strings%2C%20and%20Keyboards/","source-block-347","The work's long harmonic progression has three statements, described as variations; the catalog does not identify them as separately titled movements.",["Variations for Winds, Strings, and Keyboards"]],["Person/Steve%20Reich/Music/Work/Variations%20for%20Winds%2C%20Strings%2C%20and%20Keyboards/","source-block-501","Sources: Steve Reich work catalog; IRCAM work record.",["Variations for Winds, Strings, and Keyboards"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-42","Steve Yegge",[]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-58","About",["Steve Yegge"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-70","Engineering Leader, Sourcegraph/Amp",["Steve Yegge","About"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-110","Developer of Programming Language/Hack",["Steve Yegge","About"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-157","Online Presence",["Steve Yegge"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-179","GitHub (steveyegge)",["Steve Yegge","Online Presence"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-236","Twitter",["Steve Yegge","Online Presence"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-283","LinkedIn",["Steve Yegge","Online Presence"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-338","Medium",["Steve Yegge","Online Presence"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-384","Projects",["Steve Yegge"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-399","Beads - A memory upgrade for your coding agent",["Steve Yegge","Projects"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-454","Gastown - Multi-agent workspace manager",["Steve Yegge","Projects"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-502","Person/Steve Yegge/Blog/Welcome to Gas Town",["Steve Yegge","Projects"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-554","Person/Steve Yegge/Agent - the named crew, fleet, and role agents he runs in Wheelhouse",["Steve Yegge","Projects"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-650","Writing",["Steve Yegge"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-664","The Shape of Things to Come, a two-part essay on where agentic harnesses are heading",["Steve Yegge","Writing"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-753","Person/Steve Yegge/Blog/26/08/The Shape of Things to Come, Part 1: The Continuous Thunderdome",["Steve Yegge","Writing","The Shape of Things to Come, a two-part essay on where agentic harnesses are heading"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-856","Person/Steve Yegge/Blog/26/08/The Shape of Things to Come, Part 2: Model Welfare for Agentic Engineers",["Steve Yegge","Writing","The Shape of Things to Come, a two-part essay on where agentic harnesses are heading"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-968","Work",["Steve Yegge"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-979","Writing thousands of lines of code per day using AI coding tools",["Steve Yegge","Work"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-1048","Daily spend of $300–$500 across 3–5 Claude Code and Sourcegraph AMP sessions",["Steve Yegge","Work"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/","source-block-1137","Co-author of Person/Steve Yegge/Book/25/Vibe Coding with Person/Gene Kim",["Steve Yegge","Work"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/Agent/","source-block-0","Steve Yegge's Agents",[]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/Agent/","source-block-25","The named agents Person/Steve Yegge runs in Wheelhouse, the closed-source, bespoke harness he built for his MMO Wyvern, as described in his two-part essay The Shape of Things to Come. Wheelhouse is the harness, not an agent; so are Beads, Gastown, and the wiring below.",["Steve Yegge's Agents"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/Agent/","source-block-310","The three tiers",["Steve Yegge's Agents"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/Agent/","source-block-377","Crew — Anthropic/Model/Claude/Fable/5 work producers, 18 seats, his direct reports. They hold the long conversations, produce designs, and translate them into Beads implementation plans.",["Steve Yegge's Agents","The three tiers"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/Agent/","source-block-578","Fleet — Opus 5 work consumers, non-ephemeral successors to Gastown's polecats, each with its own repo clone, all managed by the Marshal. Every implementation bead runs Fable design, Opus implementation, Fable review.",["Steve Yegge's Agents","The three tiers"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/Agent/","source-block-805","Role agents — standing, unattended agents with named roles, running production operations. New since the Gastown days. None are Fable; a few are Opus, most are Sonnet.",["Steve Yegge's Agents","The three tiers"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/Agent/","source-block-983","In his words",["Steve Yegge's Agents","The three tiers"]],["Person/Steve%20Yegge/Agent/","source-block-1871","Role agents",["Steve Yegge's Agents"]